  "rationale": "The vignette describes a non-REM parasomnia occurring soon after sleep onset with abrupt terror and poor consolability, which is most consistent with night terrors.",
  "optionExplanations": {
    "A": "Nightmares usually occur in the second half of the night and are typically remembered on waking.",
    "B": "Obstructive sleep apnea causes snoring and breathing pauses rather than episodes of screaming and inconsolability.",
    "C": "Partial confused arousal is a parasomnia that can overlap with this pattern, but the classic diagnosis for this description is night terrors.",
    "D": "Nocturnal seizures are less likely to present with this stereotyped early-night fear episode and prolonged inconsolability.",
    "E": "Night terrors classically occur in the first third of sleep, with screaming, autonomic arousal, inconsolability, and limited recall."
